###########################
#  Main text

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
Curl is the "circulation density", i.e. the local swirl of a fluid at a point. What does this  mean? $BR
	\( \textbf{Lemma.} \) Let \( F = (f,g) : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2 \) be a \( C^1 \) vector field. Fix \( p = (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \). Then
\[
(\text{curl} F)(p) = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0^+} \frac{1}{\mathrm{area}( B_{\varepsilon}(p))} \oint_{\partial B_{\varepsilon}(p)} (F \cdot T)\,  ds
\]
where \( \partial B_{\varepsilon}(p) \) is the positively oriented circle of radius \( \varepsilon \) centered at \( p \).
$BR $BR
This can be proved with or without Green's theorem. Below is an incomplete proof with Green's theorem.
$BR
\{ image( "k2-q7-proof.png", width=>794, height=>225) \}
$BR
\{ $mc1 -> print_q() \}
\{ $mc1 -> print_a() \}

END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;


